# {{title}}

A schema for the Flux framework.

{{docker_readme}}## Running it on a server without Docker

Put this repository into `garrysmod/gamemodes` of the server, in a folder named `{{id}}`, run
`fpkg install --locked` anywhere in the server folder to install Flux and the other packages of the
schema, at the versions of `fpkg.lock`, into its `packages` folder, and start the server with
`+gamemode {{id}}`. `fpkg install` also copies the binary modules of the packages into
`garrysmod/lua/bin` and creates `garrysmod/lua/_flux`, where Flux writes generated files. The
configuration of the server goes into `gamemodes/{{id}}/config/`.

Binary modules: `gmsv_file` is installed as a dependency of `flux`. A PostgreSQL server
uncomments `s.depends 'gmsv_pg'` in `packagespec.lua` and runs `fpkg install`. MySQL and MariaDB
need mysqloo, which you copy into `garrysmod/lua/bin` by hand from
https://github.com/TeslaCloud/flux-dependencies.

Please refer to the Flux repository for the rest of the installation instructions.

## Development

Run `fpkg link <path to a flux-ce checkout>` in this checkout to use a checkout of Flux instead of
the published package, then `fpkg install` for the rest of the packages.

Commit `fpkg.lock`, so that every server installs the same versions with `fpkg install --locked`.
A lock written while flux is linked locks the checkout, which a server does not have: before you
commit it, lock the published version with `fpkg unlink flux && fpkg lock`.

The schema itself lives in `schema/`, and `{{id}}.yml` picks the plugins of Flux it loads and
sets config values. `fpkg new plugin <name>` adds a plugin to `plugins/`, which loads with the
schema, and `fpkg new package <name>` adds a package to `packages/`, which loads once
`packagespec.lua` depends on it.
